\p{The two findings of the integration itself:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{M8-F1 (fixed, \code{\verb~|Code.kval~} / \code{\verb~|Code.resume~}).} A \code{\verb~|KVAL~} whose value arrived through a frame (a suspended evaluation of the closed term) did not fill the site's inline cache; a later execution of the site evaluated the term again, and since \code{\verb~|gated~} no longer tables it that cost a tick the unsuspended run did not pay: the tick count depended on the host depth bound (2 of 8,064 declarations of the Cedar slice). The frame now carries \code{\verb~|DST_KVAL~} and the fill happens at the resume.}
\li{\strong{The gate's lanes shared two things they must not} (harness, not the machine): the store's PCHK bits (every lane has a table of its own now, \code{\verb~|M8Harness.Lane.build~}) and the binder hints of the code store (\code{\verb~|CodeStore.addSub~} -> \code{\verb~|TermStore.setBinderHint~}: lanes are engines of their own over ONE store; an audit lane elaborates in another order and its hints made the compared lanes walk terms their blocks would have run). \code{\verb~|M8Lanes~} now runs lane 0 and the convention lanes first, then the generic tier and the audits. Before that the gate of the default set reported 270 to 45,821 "different" declarations on Std and the Cedar slice, never the same twice; without \code{\verb~|code,casetrees,lazycases~} or with the lanes of one convention group alone it was clean (bisection in \code{\verb~|work/int6/bis-*.out~}). A real run has one machine engine per store; a foreign hint is validated and ignored (\code{\verb~|CodeStore.hint~}), so the effect was on ticks only.}
}
}

